<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- The plugin brokers only (docs/12 §2.3), and deliberately not the app.

	     In plain terms: library validation is macOS refusing to load code into
	     a process unless that code was signed by the same team as the process,
	     or by Apple. A third-party OFX or CLAP plugin is, by definition, signed
	     by somebody else — usually the plugin's vendor, sometimes nobody at
	     all — so a process that hosts plugins cannot have it on.

	     That is the whole reason the brokers exist. Each one is a separate
	     process that loads exactly one plugin bundle and talks to the
	     application over IPC, so the weakened containment a plugin needs is
	     spent on a process that holds no project, no media handles and no
	     window — rather than on the application, which holds all three.

	     Release.entitlements therefore keeps library validation ON: everything
	     the app itself loads (the bridge dylib, the FFmpeg libraries) is signed
	     by the same identity in packaging/macos/make-dmg.sh, so it has no need
	     of this and no business carrying it.

	     The sandbox stays off here for the same reason it is off for the app:
	     turning it on is an entitlement and file-access piece of work of its
	     own, not a line in a plist. When it is taken on, this is the process to
	     do it to first — a broker needs a pipe and one bundle path, which is
	     about as small an authority as a sandbox profile ever gets to describe.
	  -->
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<false/>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
